    fix(lang): SOL-R5 — widen the refused domain: zero-hash r"…" openers and newline-spanning strings
    
    Two parser-accepted spellings evaded the r4 substring set: the
    zero-hash KDL raw-string opener r"…" (no #"/"#/""" present) and a
    plain quoted string containing a LITERAL NEWLINE. One escape-aware
    left-to-right scan now refuses both, plus unterminated strings at EOF.
    The scan is sound because every construct that could poison its quote
    tracking (hashed raw strings, """) is refused by the substring set
    first, and it refuses AT the first remaining unprovable — tracking is
    never trusted past one. Scanner not deepened; still refusal-only.
    
    Deliberate deviation from the naive bare-substring r" rule: a plain
    string merely ENDING in the letter r contains r" at its closing quote —
    'name "system-monitor"' is real corpus content (3 fixture vertices),
    and the bare rule turns the corpus roundtrip suite red. The refusal is
    therefore position-aware (r immediately before an OPENING quote,
    outside any tracked string) — stated in the docstring; under-refusal is
    still impossible since the tracker halts at the first unprovable.
    
    Regressions (red-first): sol's two r5 repros refuse for add/edit/remove;
    multi-hash raw strings 1-8 pinned refused; string-ending-in-r happy
    path pinned allowed.
    
